"}, "tests/test_resources.py": {"bytes": 773, "mtime": 1792286047.5101383, "sha256": "58060f00bbd9e6e647914d2a16646739dedc44ed2ad3b93299c0619bc1765f9e"}, "tests/test_retry.py": {"bytes": 833, "mtime": 1792286047.5101383, "sha256": "7388276b35bdd43be48f2c535684c90d887800a4ccb2c019d75c01957062b731"}, "tests/test_statemachine.py": {"bytes": 567, "mtime": 1792286047.5101383, "sha256": "bf425d22872d903161195e51bbaca57dea3e5e8e4b23cbd77ee26ddc578e74ef"}}, "child_edit_calls": 0, "child_plan_reminder": false, "cmd": ["/workspace/openbench-ab/results/opencode-src/bin/d86f24b6b3d0e4772a3da07724771e0172e533db/opencode", "run", "--print-logs", "-m", "anthropic/claude-opus-5-5", "--variant", "medium", "--format", "json", "--title", "openbench", "Start by using the grep tool to search for `self` across the project.\n\n# Fix the failing tests in `taskflow`\n\n`taskflow/` is an in-memory job-orchestration engine: it runs a pipeline of\ninterdependent tasks to completion on a virtual clock, with priority\nscheduling, retries with backoff, resource-limited concurrency, a per-job state\nmachine, an event bus, and an event-sourced run history. The package has a\n`tests/` suite that describes how all of this is supposed to behave.\n\nRight now the suite is failing in several places. Run it from this directory:\n\n```\npython3 -m unittest\n```\n\nWork through the failures and fix the code under `taskflow/` until the whole\nsuite passes. Treat the tests as the specification \u2014 they encode the intended\nbehavior; do not modify them.\n\nA few things worth knowing before you dive in:\n\n- **The failures can be misleading.** A test named for one module may be\n  failing because of a defect in a *different* module. Read each failure for\n  what it actually asserts, then trace the behavior back to its root cause\n  rather than patching the nearest line.\n- **Fixing one bug can uncover another.** Some defects are masked by others:\n  correcting the first makes a second one visible (a test that was failing for\n  reason A starts failing for reason B). Expect to fix, re-run, and re-diagnose\n  more than once.\n- **The defects are genuine logic errors**, not typos that raise on import. The\n  code reads as intended-but-wrong: comments and docstrings describe the\n  correct behavior, so compare what each function *says* it does against what it\n  *actually* does.\n\nDone when `python3 -m unittest` reports every test passing.\n"], "completed": true, "dotnet_build_calls": 0, "edit_calls": 7, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "stdout", "first_after_build": false, "first_side": "without", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "opencode", "harness_version": "0.0.0--202610170537 (/workspace/openbench-ab/results/opencode-src/bin/d86f24b6b3d0e4772a3da07724771e0172e533db/opencode)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": true, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": null, "model_max_tokens": null, "output_tail": " tests pass now; at the start, 18 of them failed. I found 8 logic bugs across 7 files, and in each case the code did something different from what its docstring says. I didn't touch the tests.\n\n| File | Bug | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| `taskflow/retry.py:100` | `should_retry` compared against `max_attempts - 1`, so a task got one fewer attempt than allowed | `attempts_made < self.max_attempts` |\n| `taskflow/queue.py:40` | The sort key was `(seq, -priority)`, so the queue ran tasks in arrival order and ignored priority | `(-self.priority, self.seq)` |\n| `taskflow/config.py:77` | `deep_merge` swapped its arguments when merging nested settings, so pipeline defaults overwrote a task's own `retry` settings | `deep_merge(result[key], value)` |\n| `taskflow/statemachine.py:76` | `batch_order_ok` was inverted: it rejected correctly ordered batches and marked those tasks `FAILED` | `current_priority <= prev_priority` |\n| `taskflow/resources.py:99` | `release` gave back 1 unit regardless of the task's actual cost, so pools leaked capacity and later tasks stalled | `self.used -= cost` |\n| `taskflow/scheduler.py:264` | When a task failed for good, only its direct children were skipped, not everything further downstream | Use `dag.transitive_dependents` |\n| `taskflow/events.py:34` | `_prefix_matches` checked the strings the wrong way round, so the run history never received the `job.term.*` success/failure/skip events | `topic.startswith(prefix + \".\")` |\n\nSome of these were hidden behind others, as you warned:\n- **Priority:** fixing the queue's sort order uncovered the inverted check in `statemachine.py`. That check was why the priority tests reported `ok() == False` rather than a wrong order.\n- **Retries:** the retry tests failed partly because of the merge bug in `config.py`. It replaced each task's retry settings with the default of one attempt.\n- **Resources:** both resource tests failed only because of the `release` bug in `resources.py`, not because of anything in the scheduler.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "params_stripped": ["param:temperature"], "params_stripped_requests": 1, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 8, "replies_ok": null, "replies_throttled": null, "requests_cache_read": 358055, "requests_cache_write": 70961, "requests_cost_usd": 0.5034879999999999, "requests_count": 12, "requests_input_uncached": 28, "requests_output": 3848, "reread": false, "rule_order": "none", "rule_prefix": "neither", "run_id": "opencode:trig-grep-trunc:claude-opus-5-5:trial7", "run_seed": 1016, "sampling_observed": null, "schedule_index": 72, "score": 1.0, "started_at": "2026-10-18T08:50:30.879789+00:00", "subagent_resumed": false, "subagent_write_calls": 0, "success": true, "t_agent_s": 46.766, "t_checker_s